    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe addition follows the class's established pattern precisely. `Owner` already exposes a family of derived, `@Transient`, read-only presentation getters \u2014 `getInitials()`, the membership-number getter, and `getCustomerCode()` (which itself does region derivation and SHA-256 hashing inline). `getTelephoneDisplay()` slots in as one more cohesive member of that same family: a single derived getter over an existing persisted field, documented in the same Javadoc style, and mirrored in `openapi.yml` with a `readOnly` schema entry exactly like its siblings (`customerCode` sits right below it with the identical shape).\n\nA few things I checked and found clean:\n- **Right unit.** Given the existing precedent of derived display getters living on the entity, a maintainer would look here for this, not in a separate formatter. It's consistent with the codebase, not a new convention.\n- **No duplication.** The grouping loop and country-code split are self-contained; nothing else in the diff (or the referenced `customerCode` \"normalized telephone\" logic) formats phone numbers for display, so there's nothing to reuse or collide with.\n- **Not a catch-all.** The method does one thing (E.164 \u2192 human display) and stays within one method; it doesn't bloat an unrelated method or stuff presentation concerns into persistence code any more than the surrounding getters already do.\n- **Doc/spec coherence.** The Javadoc and the OpenAPI description agree on the contract and both reaffirm that raw `telephone` stays E.164, matching the spec's \"keep 'telephone' as raw E.164.\"\n\nI'm deliberately not judging the country-code heuristic's correctness or the choice to hand-roll parsing vs. a library \u2014 those are correctness/design calls outside this review's scope. Structurally, this is additive, idiomatic, and consistent with how the class is already organized.",
